<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Backup + transfer rules, Android 12 (API 31) and above.

    WHAT IS AT STAKE: the `translation` table stores `source_text` and
    `target_text` — literally the sentences the user typed to be translated
    (TranslationEntity). With the stock template (every rule commented out) all of
    it was copied to the user's Google Drive. Passwords, medical notes and private
    messages get pasted into translators; that content must not leave the device
    because we never made a decision.

    THE DECISION (docs/plan/launch-blockers.md §4):
      * cloud-backup   — the database is EXCLUDED. Nothing the user typed is
                         copied off the device.
      * device-transfer — the database is INCLUDED. D2D moves data directly
                         between two phones the same person is holding; keeping
                         history through a phone upgrade is the behaviour users
                         expect, and it never reaches a third party.

    Android supports exactly this split: "exclude a file or directory from Google
    Drive backups while still transferring it during device-to-device (D2D)
    transfers" — developer.android.com/identity/data/autobackup.

    These are EXCLUDE-ONLY rule sets on purpose. Adding a single <include> would
    switch each section to allow-list mode and silently drop everything else, so
    DataStore preferences (theme, language pair, mobile-data consent, usage
    counters) keep their default backed-up behaviour.

    The journals are named explicitly: they live beside the database in the same
    `database` domain and hold recently written rows, so excluding only the `.db`
    would leak the newest translations. All three journal suffixes are listed
    because Room's default `JournalMode.AUTOMATIC` resolves to WAL (-wal/-shm) on
    most devices but to TRUNCATE (-journal) on low-RAM ones — the file we do not
    name is the one that leaks.

    BOTH databases are excluded. This release upgrades v1.0.4 IN PLACE (same
    applicationId), and that build wrote its own `translator.db`
    (Tranzlate DatabaseModule DBNAME, created from a bundled asset so the file is
    present on EVERY install). We do not read it, but it survives the upgrade in
    this same `database` domain carrying the old app's typed source/target text —
    so excluding only our file would keep every upgraded user's old history
    flowing to Drive and make the promise above false for exactly the people this
    release ships to. It is excluded, NOT deleted: it is the only copy of that
    history, and an importer is follow-up work.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <exclude domain="database" path="tranzlate.db" />
        <exclude domain="database" path="tranzlate.db-wal" />
        <exclude domain="database" path="tranzlate.db-shm" />
        <exclude domain="database" path="tranzlate.db-journal" />
        <exclude domain="database" path="translator.db" />
        <exclude domain="database" path="translator.db-wal" />
        <exclude domain="database" path="translator.db-shm" />
        <exclude domain="database" path="translator.db-journal" />
    </cloud-backup>
    <!--
        Declared even though it carries no exclusions: an ABSENT <device-transfer>
        section already means "everything", so writing it down is what makes the
        difference between the two modes a decision instead of an accident.
    -->
    <device-transfer />
</data-extraction-rules>
